This index should help speed up queries that filter by symbol or date, such as the one you mentioned.

Case 5:

The results of this test are ambiguous because they don't take into account the actual query execution time. The `microtime()` function in PHP is not very accurate for measuring small intervals of time.

To get a more accurate measurement of the query execution time, you can use the `getmicrotime()` function from the `sys_get_microtime()` function in PHP:

```php
list($startMicroTime1, $startMicroTime2) = sys_get_microtime();
// your code here
list($endMicroTime1, $endMicroTime2) = sys_get_microtime();

$startTime = $startMicroTime1 + 0.000001 * $startMicroTime2;
$endTime = $endMicroTime1 + 0.000001 * $endMicroTime2;

$totalTime = ($endTime - $startTime);
```

This will give you a more accurate measurement of the query execution time.

As for why having the wildcard at the end of the search string would be better practice than the beginning, it's because SQL Server can use an index to speed up queries that have wildcards at the end of the search string. This is because the database can quickly locate the desired data by scanning the index from right to left.

On the other hand, having the wildcard at the beginning of the search string means that the database has to scan the entire table or index to find the desired data, which can be slower.
